 "source": {
  "author": "PingYou Ltd",
  "kind": "new finite construction",
  "attribution_note": "Classical regular hexagonal honeycomb, proper cube symmetries and guillotine composition. No finite published donor packing. PingYou Ltd supplies the heterogeneous finite whole-patch return words and their complete certificates.",
  "construction": {
   "name": "heterogeneous_complete_honeycomb_patch_rows_and_layers",
   "proof": "Whole honeycomb patches with explicit end costs and cyclic layer composition\n\nUse the canonical regular hexagonal prism of base edge one and height L, with planar vertices (+-1,0),(+-1/2,+-sqrt(3)/2). Its standard honeycomb translation lattice has basis (3/2,sqrt(3)/2),(0,sqrt(3)). For positive integers a,b take all ab whole owners with centers\n  (3i/2, sqrt(3)*(j+(i mod 2)/2), 0), 0<=i<a, 0<=j<b.\nThese are lattice words (i,j-floor(i/2)); their interiors are disjoint because the lattice tiles the plane by the whole regular hexagons. Extrusion through the full prism height preserves this separation.\n\nThe exact patch box has dimensions\n  d0=(3a+1)/2,\n  d1=sqrt(3)*(b+epsilon/2), epsilon=1 if a>1, otherwise0,\n  d2=L.\nThe patch's box midpoint is\n  (3(a-1)/4, sqrt(3)*(b-1+epsilon/2)/2,0).\nIn particular both transverse end costs are retained; ab is not treated as a fractional area density. The two endpoint phases of the staggered columns remain present, including a final unmatched column when a is odd.\n\nTreat the complete patch as a module and apply the three proper cyclic coordinate permutations. Type k makes intrinsic axis k vertical, with the other two axes ordered cyclically. At a horizontal event E the module-grid capacity per layer is floor(E/d_i)*floor(E/d_j). A layer word (l0,l1,l2) has actual height H=sum(lk*dk), owner count ab times the sum of those capacities weighted by lk, and common enclosing cube edge S=max(E,H). Place all layers at consecutive closed intervals and center each complete transverse grid. Modules in different grid cells or layers have disjoint box interiors. Within each module the actual honeycomb lattice gives pair separators. A final suffix of complete owners may be deleted, with every parent and retained word recorded.\n\nThe finite probe compares complete patch dimensions and integer layer words below the current public/submitted/private target. No metric angle is varied. Proper cyclic quaternions and the canonical owner orientation are fixed before the probe. A prospective gain is promoted only after reconstructing every owner and independently checking the source, all native wall constraints and all pairs. Equal-value and nongain patch states remain controls, not global optimality claims.\n\nHeterogeneous whole-patch rows and layers\n\nThis extends HONEYCOMB_LAYER_PROOF.txt by composing different complete honeycomb patches rather than requiring a single patch shape in every layer. Each module has actual ab whole hexagonal-prism owners, with the same source lattice word and full patch boundary costs as before. A fixed proper signed axis permutation puts its bounding box dimensions into (width,row-height,layer-height).\n\nFor one exact layer-height h, a row type consists of floor(S/width) identical complete modules at that fixed frame, centered across a common width S. Its cost is its full row-height, and its value is ab times the module count. A row word with summed heights at most S gives an actual layer with that many whole owners: place the closed row intervals consecutively, center the combined rows, and retain their different module widths. Distinct rows have disjoint box interiors.\n\nA layer word with summed full layer-heights at most S then gives a complete cube packing, by placing those layers on consecutive closed intervals and centering the stack. Different layers have disjoint box interiors. Every owner in a module has the inherited honeycomb orientation, further acted on by the module's fixed proper cube symmetry. The grammar's max-plus count is therefore constructive: its full backpointer word is an inverse decoder, not only an upper or density estimate. Deleting a named whole-owner suffix preserves a complete packing.\n\nAll costs lie in (p+q*sqrt(3))/2 with nonnegative integers p,q. The finite dynamic program stores (p,q) costs and an actual word, first for rows at one layer height, then for layers. The metric comparison only orders those exact positive costs against the common cube side; no angle or independent physical scale is optimized. A source module that has no count advantage may still occur in an equal-value boundary word and is retained in the candidate bank. The final native witness verifies all actual vertices and pair separation independently of the grammar.\n",
   "cost_convention": "Each integer pair[p,q]denotes(p+q*sqrt(3))/2. Every module cost includes both complete patch ends.",
